'''''Paspalum vaginatum''''' is a species of [[Poaceae|grass]] known by many names, including '''seashore paspalum''', '''biscuit grass''', '''saltwater couch''', '''silt grass''', and '''swamp couch'''.<ref name=pier>[http://www.hear.org/pier/species/paspalum_vaginatum.htm ''Paspalum vaginatum''.] Pacific Island Ecosystems at Risk.</ref> It is native to the Americas, where it grows in tropical and subtropical regions.<ref name=duble>Duble, R. L. [http://aggie-horticulture.tamu.edu/archives/parsons/turf/publications/seashore.html Seashore Paspalum.] Aggie Horticulture. Texas A&M System.</ref> It is found throughout the other tropical areas of the world, where it is an [[introduced species]] and sometimes an [[invasive species|invasive weed]].<ref name=pier/> It is also cultivated as a [[Lawn|turfgrass]] in many places.<ref name=ralish>Ralish, G. [http://grove.ufl.edu/~turf/turfcult/ralish.html ''Paspalum vaginatum'': An alternative to bermudagrass.] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110907193152/http://grove.ufl.edu/~turf/turfcult/ralish.html |date=2011-09-07 }} University of Florida Turf Science.</ref>

==Description==
It is a perennial grass with [[rhizome]]s or [[stolon]]s. The stems grow {{convert|10|to|79|cm}} tall. The leaf blades are {{convert|10|to|19|cm}} long and may be hairless to slightly hairy.<ref name=gm>[http://herbarium.usu.edu/webmanual/info2.asp?name=Paspalum_vaginatum&type=treatment ''Paspalum vaginatum''.] {{webarchive|url=https://archive.today/20121211123319/http://herbarium.usu.edu/webmanual/info2.asp?name=Paspalum_vaginatum&type=treatment |date=2012-12-11 }} Grass Manual Treatment.</ref> They are usually blue-green in color.<ref name=usda>[https://plants.usda.gov/factsheet/pdf/fs_pava.pdf ''Paspalum vaginatum''.] USDA NRCS Plant Fact Sheet.</ref> The [[panicle]] is usually a pair of branches up to {{convert|7.9|cm}} long; there is sometimes a third branch below the pair. The branches are lined with oval to lance-shaped spikelets which grow pressed against the branches, making the panicle narrow.<ref name=gm/> This species is similar to [[Cynodon|bermudagrass]].<ref name=usda/><ref name=bros>Brosnan, J. T. and J. Deputy. [http://www.ctahr.hawaii.edu/oc/freepubs/pdf/TM-1.pdf Seashore Paspalum.] Cooperative Extension, [[University of Hawaiʻi at Mānoa]].</ref> It spreads by its rhizomes and stolons, forming a thick [[Lawn|turf]].<ref name=usda/><ref name=usda2>[https://plants.usda.gov/plantguide/pdf/pg_pava.pdf ''Paspalum vaginatum''.] USDA NRCS Plant Guide.</ref> In the wild this species grows in [[salt marsh]]es and [[brackish marsh]]es.<ref name=gm/> The genome of ''Paspalum vaginatum'' was sequenced in 2022 and the species' haploid genome was determined to be approximately 590 megabases in size.<ref name="Sun Wase Shu Jenkins 2022 p. ">{{cite journal | last=Sun | first=Guangchao | last2=Wase | first2=Nishikant | last3=Shu | first3=Shengqiang | last4=Jenkins | first4=Jerry | last5=Zhou | first5=Bangjun | last6=Torres-Rodríguez | first6=J. Vladimir | last7=Chen | first7=Cindy | last8=Sandor | first8=Laura | last9=Plott | first9=Chris | last10=Yoshinga | first10=Yuko | last11=Daum | first11=Christopher | last12=Qi | first12=Peng | last13=Barry | first13=Kerrie | last14=Lipzen | first14=Anna | last15=Berry | first15=Luke | last16=Pedersen | first16=Connor | last17=Gottilla | first17=Thomas | last18=Foltz | first18=Ashley | last19=Yu | first19=Huihui | last20=O’Malley | first20=Ronan | last21=Zhang | first21=Chi | last22=Devos | first22=Katrien M. | last23=Sigmon | first23=Brandi | last24=Yu | first24=Bin | last25=Obata | first25=Toshihiro | last26=Schmutz | first26=Jeremy | last27=Schnable | first27=James C. | title=Genome of Paspalum vaginatum and the role of trehalose mediated autophagy in increasing maize biomass | journal=Nature Communications | volume=13 | issue=1 | date=2022-12-13 | issn=2041-1723 | doi=10.1038/s41467-022-35507-8 | page=| doi-access=free | pmc=9747981 }}</ref>

==Uses==
This grass has been bred into [[cultivar]]s which are used for [[golf course turf]] and other [[landscaping]] projects. It forms a higher quality turf than bermudagrass in poor conditions, such as wet soils and low light levels, and with fewer nitrogen [[Soil conditioner|soil amendment]]s.<ref name=duble/><ref name=bros/> It can grow in lawns that receive rain on 250 days per year, and it can survive being waterlogged or submerged for several days at a time.<ref name=ralish/> It tolerates foot traffic.<ref name=usda2/> The main advantage of this grass is that it is very salt-tolerant. It can be [[irrigation|irrigated]] with non-potable water, such as [[greywater]], an important advantage in a time when there are increasing restrictions on water use.<ref name=bros/> The grass can even be irrigated with [[saline water|saltwater]].<ref name=bros/><ref name=usda2/> The grass will be lower in quality than that irrigated with potable water, but it survives.<ref name=bros/> This species has "filled a niche in America", being a better grass to use on turf next to the ocean in places that receive [[sea spray]].<ref name=ralish/> "It has the highest salt tolerance of all turfgrasses."<ref name=ralish/> It is also more competitive against weeds than similar grasses.<ref name=ralish/> Weeds can be controlled by the application of saltwater, in which weeds will die and the grass will survive.<ref name=zul>Zulkaliph, N. A., et al. (2011). [http://www.docstoc.com/docs/81352826/Use-of-saline-water-for-weed-control-in-seashore-Paspalum-%28Paspalum-vaginatum%29 Use of saline water for weed control in seashore Paspalum (''Paspalum vaginatum'').] ''Australian Journal of Crop Science'' 5 523-30.</ref>

There are many cultivars bred for various uses. These cultivars are highly variable. For example, fine-textured types are used for golf courses, while coarser grass is selected for roadside revegetation.<ref name=bros/> The best-known and oldest cultivar may be 'Adalayd'<ref name=ralish/> a grass with a similar texture and blue-green color to [[Kentucky bluegrass]].<ref name=duble/> The cultivar 'Aloha' is a dark green grass used for golf courses and [[athletic field]]s. 'Salam', the most common cultivar in [[Hawaii]], is another dark green type suitable for golf courses.<ref name=bros/> The leaves of 'Salam' are glossy on the undersides, giving the field a striped look when it is mowed.<ref name=usda2/> 'Seadwarf' is a bright green type that is more resistant to a grass disease called [[dollar spot]] than other cultivars.<ref name=bros/>

There are other uses for the grass, including [[erosion]] control in sandy coastal areas, its native habitat. It can be used in [[phytoremediation]] when the soils are high in heavy metals.<ref name=usda/> It has been shown to survive pollution with [[crude oil]].<ref name=bam>Bamidele, J. F. and A. Igiri. (2011). [http://www.ajol.info/index.php/jasem/article/viewFile/68514/56592 Growth of seashore paspalum, (''Paspalum vaginatum'' L) in soil contaminated with crude petroleum oil.] ''J. Appl. Sci. Environ. Manage.'' 15(2) 303-306.</ref> It can be used to feed [[livestock]] and some wild herbivores will graze it.<ref name=usda2/>

[[Truist Park]], the home [[ballpark]] of the [[Atlanta Braves]], has Seashore Paspalum Platinum TE as its field surface.

